#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="The pure decision behind plan-hold-guard: is a background hold live, so a turn that hands control to a human will not end idle?"
#
# CLOUD-451. Handing control to a human — `ExitPlanMode`, `AskUserQuestion` — is
# the one turn end that is CORRECTLY idle: nothing is running because nothing
# should be, the session is waiting on a person. The container reclaims idle
# sessions anyway, and the cost lands on the human rather than the agent: text
# already typed into the approval box is discarded, or the plan closes and
# reopens empty. The longer someone reads before approving, the likelier their
# input is destroyed, which makes careful review the *punished* behaviour.
#
# `run-shape-guard` already names the failure ("an idle turn gets the VM
# reclaimed") and AGENTS.md carries it as a rule. Both are about the agent's own
# idleness and neither reaches this boundary, so the rule shipped without its
# mechanism (non-negotiable 2). `plan-hold` is the mechanism; this is its
# predicate, split out for the same reason `memory-guard-check` is split from
# `memory-guard`: the decision is then directly callable and directly testable,
# and the sentinel location is spelled exactly once, here.
#
# **A hold is live when a sentinel names a pid that still answers.** Two parts,
# never one. A bare "the file exists" would let a killed hold — a reclaimed
# container, a `land` that took the process down — read as protection that is not
# there, which is worse than no guard at all because it is silent. This is the
# same corpse-versus-free-lock distinction `alive` draws over `batten-tasks` and
# `target-ensure` draws over its lock, and the asymmetry is chosen the same way:
# here a dead hold read as LIVE is the expensive direction, because it licenses
# exactly the idle handoff this exists to prevent. So an unevaluable probe reads
# as dead, and the cost of being wrong is one redundant `mise run plan-hold`.
#
# **Reporting a corpse also reaps it** (again `alive`'s shape), so the hold
# directory is self-healing rather than an accumulating pile of headstones that
# would each need a `kill -0` on every future call.
#
# One sentinel per PID, not one per session. `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID` is not reliably
# exported into a task's environment, and a single per-repo path would have two
# concurrent sessions overwriting each other's record. A file named by pid needs
# no identity plumbing at all, and "is anything holding this clone open" is the
# question the guard actually asks.
#
# **The hold also records WHY IT STOPPED, and that is a second predicate**
# (CLOUD-491). `live` answers about now; `spanned` answers about the last
# container replacement, which is the question CLOUD-451's acceptance is written
# in and which nothing could previously check after the fact.
#
# The obvious sensor — a per-poll timestamp, compared against boot time — was
# built and measured, and it cannot work here. Every write that survived the
# 2026-08-12 23:45 replacement stops at 23:42:15 (four MCP servers' logs,
# Serena's log, `target/.rustc_info.json`, `batten-holds`) while the container
# kept serving tool calls until ~23:45: **the last 182 s of writes did not
# survive.** Snapshot boundary or unflushed page cache cannot be told apart from
# inside, and it does not matter — the tail of a heartbeat is lost exactly when
# the sensor needs it, so any `epoch >= btime - poll` reads three minutes stale
# and answers "did not span" for the event it exists to catch. Widening the
# tolerance to 182 s swallows a hold that legitimately exited minutes earlier,
# which collapses the case the whole thing exists to distinguish.
#
# So the record is STRUCTURAL, not temporal: `h` per poll, `x` on an intentional
# exit, and the verdict is the LAST LINE's kind. A hold killed by the container
# going down cannot write an `x`, so an `h` in final position IS the evidence —
# and absence of a record is the one reading a lost tail cannot forge. The
# residual error is bounded and points the safe way: a hold released inside the
# lost-write window reads as "was live".
#
# Usage: plan-hold-check live      — exit 0 live / 1 not live / 2 cannot look
#        plan-hold-check dir       — print the hold directory (for plan-hold and
#                                    plan-hold-release, so the path has one home)
#        plan-hold-check spanned   — exit 0 a hold was live when this container
#                                    replaced the last one / 1 it was not / 2
#                                    cannot look
#        plan-hold-check heartbeat-path — print the heartbeat file (for plan-hold,
#                                    so this path also has one home)
#        plan-hold-check poll      — print the poll interval, so the sleeper and
#                                    this reader cannot disagree about it
#        plan-hold-check boot      — print this container's boot time
#        plan-hold-check record-boot — note this boot, so a later `spanned` can
#                                    tell a preserved disk from a fresh one
#
# Exit codes are §7's: 0 the predicate holds, 1 it does not, 2 the question could
# not be asked. There is deliberately no exit code for "reaped something" — that
# is bookkeeping, not a verdict.
#
# The mutation collapses the two record kinds into one, which is exactly the
# reading the 182 s measurement rules out: with `h` and `x` indistinguishable the
# only thing left to reason from is the clock, and the clock is stale by minutes
# at the moment it is asked. Declared at column 0 because that is what `mutant`'s
# `sed -n 's/^#MUTANT //p'` reads, and because shfmt re-indents a comment placed
# beside the code inside a `case` arm.
#MUTANT kind-discrimination-collapses|s/if \[ "$kind" = "x" \]; then/if false; then/|an x means the hold ended on purpose
set -uo pipefail

# The one authoritative spelling of the hold location. Under `$GIT_DIR` rather
# than `$TMPDIR` because that is where this repo already keeps per-clone
# machinery (`batten-receipts`, `batten-tasks`), and because `claim-guard`
# already treats the git dir as machinery rather than work — so a hold can be
# armed inside plan mode without a write to any tracked path.
# ABSOLUTE, deliberately: `--git-dir` answers `.git` from the repo root, and this
# path is consumed by hooks whose working directory is the harness's rather than
# the one the hold was armed from. A relative answer would have two callers
# resolving the same hold to different directories, which is a false not-live —
# the reading that licenses the idle handoff.
hold_dir() {
	local git_dir
	git_dir=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null) || return 1
	[ -n "$git_dir" ] || return 1
	printf '%s\n' "$git_dir/batten-holds"
}

# BESIDE the hold directory, never inside it. Both `plan-hold`'s
# incumbent-release loop and the reaping loop below iterate `"$dir"/*` and delete
# every file whose first line is not a pid — so a heartbeat filed under the hold
# directory would be reaped by the very check that reads it. Measured on the
# first draft of CLOUD-491, before a line of it was written.
heartbeat_file() {
	local git_dir
	git_dir=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null) || return 1
	[ -n "$git_dir" ] || return 1
	printf '%s\n' "$git_dir/batten-hold-heartbeat"
}

# One line per session start, so `spanned` can tell "no hold was ever armed on
# this disk" from "this disk is new and no local evidence can exist". The naive
# probe — look for prior-container residue — does not work: a `batten-*`
# directory's mtime updates on every add and remove, so all of them read as
# post-boot on a demonstrably preserved disk, and `target/` is rebuilt at boot on
# a fresh one. A btime we wrote ourselves is a reading rather than an inference.
boots_file() {
	local git_dir
	git_dir=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null) || return 1
	[ -n "$git_dir" ] || return 1
	printf '%s\n' "$git_dir/batten-boots"
}

# The poll interval's one home. `plan-hold` asks for it rather than spelling its
# own default, for the reason the hold directory has one spelling: two literals
# that must agree are a drift waiting to happen, and here the disagreement would
# be silent — the sleeper polling at one rate while the reader reasons about
# another.
POLL_DEFAULT=5

# `/proc/stat`'s btime is the container's boot time in epoch seconds. Injectable
# because it CANNOT BE FAKED otherwise, and a suite that cannot vary the boot
# time cannot exercise a single row of the table below — it would ship as
# coverage while discriminating nothing (CLOUD-418).
boot_time() {
	local bt
	# Set at all, not merely non-empty: an override that is present is
	# AUTHORITATIVE, so a malformed one is "cannot look" rather than a silent
	# fall-through to /proc/stat. Falling through would make the override look
	# honoured while the reading came from somewhere else — the shape of a test
	# that passes without exercising anything.
	if [ -n "${BATTEN_BOOT_TIME+set}" ]; then
		case "$BATTEN_BOOT_TIME" in
		'' | *[!0-9]*) return 1 ;;
		esac
		printf '%s\n' "$BATTEN_BOOT_TIME"
		return 0
	fi
	bt=$(awk '$1 == "btime" { print $2; exit }' /proc/stat 2>/dev/null)
	case "$bt" in
	'' | *[!0-9]*) ;;
	*)
		printf '%s\n' "$bt"
		return 0
		;;
	esac
	# `uptime -s` answers a wall-clock string rather than an epoch; converting it
	# is what makes it a fallback rather than a second format to reason about.
	bt=$(date -u -d "$(uptime -s 2>/dev/null)" +%s 2>/dev/null)
	case "$bt" in
	'' | *[!0-9]*) return 1 ;;
	esac
	printf '%s\n' "$bt"
}

mode="${1:-live}"

case "$mode" in
dir)
	dir=$(hold_dir) || {
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: not a git repository — no hold directory" >&2
		exit 2
	}
	printf '%s\n' "$dir"
	exit 0
	;;
heartbeat-path)
	beat=$(heartbeat_file) || {
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: not a git repository — no heartbeat file" >&2
		exit 2
	}
	printf '%s\n' "$beat"
	exit 0
	;;
poll)
	printf '%s\n' "${BATTEN_PLAN_HOLD_POLL:-$POLL_DEFAULT}"
	exit 0
	;;
boot)
	boot=$(boot_time) || {
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: cannot read this container's boot time" >&2
		exit 2
	}
	printf '%s\n' "$boot"
	exit 0
	;;
record-boot)
	boot=$(boot_time) || {
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: cannot read this container's boot time — not recording it" >&2
		exit 2
	}
	boots=$(boots_file) || {
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: not a git repository — cannot record this boot" >&2
		exit 2
	}
	# Idempotent by the LAST line, not by a search: a session that restarts
	# within one container must not add a second record, and a genuinely earlier
	# boot recurring is not a case that can arise — btime only moves forward.
	last=$(tail -n 1 -- "$boots" 2>/dev/null)
	[ "$last" = "$boot" ] || printf '%s\n' "$boot" >>"$boots" 2>/dev/null || true
	exit 0
	;;
spanned)
	boot=$(boot_time) || {
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: cannot read this container's boot time — cannot say whether a hold spanned the restart" >&2
		exit 2
	}
	beat=$(heartbeat_file) || {
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: not a git repository — cannot say whether a hold spanned the restart" >&2
		exit 2
	}
	# `-s`, so an empty file reads as absence rather than as a corrupt record.
	# A hold that armed and died before its first poll leaves exactly that.
	if [ ! -s "$beat" ]; then
		boots=$(boots_file) || boots=""
		prior=""
		if [ -n "$boots" ] && [ -r "$boots" ]; then
			while IFS= read -r recorded; do
				case "$recorded" in
				'' | *[!0-9]*) continue ;;
				esac
				[ "$recorded" = "$boot" ] || prior="$recorded"
			done <"$boots"
		fi
		if [ -n "$prior" ]; then
			echo "plan-hold-check: no hold has ever been armed in this clone (boot $boot, disk carries boot $prior)"
			exit 1
		fi
		# No heartbeat AND no boot but this one: nothing that ran here left a
		# trace, which is what a fresh disk looks like. Reporting "no hold was
		# live" would be a claim the evidence cannot support, and it is the
		# claim that would license the idle handoff.
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: no record predates boot $boot — this container's disk carries no evidence either way" >&2
		exit 2
	fi

	last=$(tail -n 1 -- "$beat" 2>/dev/null) || last=""
	# Fields, never a regex: `read` splits on IFS and the shapes below are a
	# fixed arity, so there is nothing for a pattern to get subtly wrong.
	read -r kind epoch recorded _rest <<<"$last"
	case "$kind" in
	h | x) ;;
	*)
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: the last heartbeat record is unreadable — cannot say whether a hold spanned the restart" >&2
		exit 2
		;;
	esac
	case "$epoch$recorded" in
	'' | *[!0-9]*)
		echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: the last heartbeat record carries no usable timestamps — cannot say whether a hold spanned the restart" >&2
		exit 2
		;;
	esac

	# THE WHOLE PREDICATE, in three lines. An `x` is written only where the hold
	# CHOSE to stop, so its presence in final position settles the question; a
	# record from this boot never spanned anything; and an `h` left in final
	# position by a previous boot is a hold that never got to write its `x`.
	if [ "$kind" = "x" ]; then
		echo "plan-hold-check: the last hold ended on purpose at $epoch — it did not span a restart"
		exit 1
	fi
	if [ "$recorded" = "$boot" ]; then
		echo "plan-hold-check: the last heartbeat is from this boot ($boot) — nothing spanned a restart"
		exit 1
	fi
	echo "plan-hold-check: a hold was live when this container replaced the last one (last beat $epoch under boot $recorded, now $boot)"
	exit 0
	;;
live) ;;
*)
	echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: unknown mode \"$mode\" (want: live | dir | spanned | heartbeat-path | poll | boot | record-boot)" >&2
	exit 2
	;;
esac

dir=$(hold_dir) || {
	echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: not a git repository — cannot say whether a hold is live" >&2
	exit 2
}

# The refusal text, written once. Both the not-live exits below reach it, so the
# fix a caller is told about cannot drift between them — a deny that does not
# carry its fix is the shape `refusal.rs` exists to make impossible, and this is
# the shell side keeping the same bargain.
#
# Pointer-only (non-negotiable 4): a command and a path. Never a plan, never a
# question, never a line of whatever the session was about to ask.
not_live() {
	cat <<'FIX'
No background hold is live, so ending this turn would leave the container idle
while a human reads — and an idle container is reclaimed, destroying whatever
they had already typed.

Arm one first, in its own tool call, with run_in_background:

  mise run plan-hold

It prints nothing until it is released, costs no tokens while it waits, and
exits within one poll of the human's reply — that exit is the wake-up. Bypass
with BATTEN_PLAN_HOLD_BYPASS=1 when ending the turn idle is genuinely intended.
FIX
	exit 1
}

# Never created, so nothing has ever held this clone open. That is a real answer
# — not live — and NOT the same as being unable to look, which is exit 2 above.
[ -d "$dir" ] || not_live

# Present but unreadable is "could not look". Distinguished from absence for the
# same reason `alive` distinguishes them: collapsing the two would report a
# broken registry as a confident verdict.
[ -r "$dir" ] && [ -x "$dir" ] || {
	echo "::error:: plan-hold-check: hold directory at $dir is unreadable — cannot say whether a hold is live" >&2
	exit 2
}

live=0
# Lexical glob order, so repeated calls over the same state are byte-stable
# (non-negotiable 5) rather than dependent on readdir order.
for file in "$dir"/*; do
	[ -f "$file" ] || continue
	pid=$(head -n 1 -- "$file" 2>/dev/null) || continue
	# A half-written record is not a hold. Inventing one from a blank or a
	# non-numeric line would be a claim, so it is reaped like a corpse.
	case "$pid" in
	'' | *[!0-9]*)
		rm -f -- "$file" 2>/dev/null || true
		continue
		;;
	esac
	if kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then
		live=$((live + 1))
	else
		rm -f -- "$file" 2>/dev/null || true
	fi
done

[ "$live" -gt 0 ] || not_live

# Pointer-only on the allow path too: a count, never a pid list and never what
# the hold is holding for.
echo "plan-hold-check: $live hold(s) live"
